⍝ test.kap — Kap-native test framework for the Rust port.
⍝ Loaded first by the kap-test runner (use "test/test.kap").
⍝ Mirrors the assertion vocabulary of the Kotlin *Test.kt harness
⍝ (assert1DArray / InnerBigIntOrLong / NearDouble / NearComplex), expressed in Kap
⍝ so it runs against the real engine under test. Strategy §9b D5 / §10.
⍝ Tests assert OBSERVABLE behaviour only.
⍝
⍝ Correct Kap semantics (docs/reference.asciidoc):
⍝   - `throw` monadic -> exception type :error
⍝   - `catch` is `F catch ((tag, fn), ...)`; handler gets (data, tag)
⍝   - `if (c) {t} else {e}`

⍝ ---- counters (read by the host from the TESTS summary line) ----
testTotal ← 0
testPass  ← 0
testFail  ← 0

⍝ fail if a and b are not tolerantly equal.
∇ assertEqual(a;b) {
  (~a≡b) : throw "assertEqual failed"
}

⍝ float/complex tolerance: |a-b| ≤ 10^-p
∇ assertNear(a;b;p) {
  |a-b > 10*-p : throw "assertNear failed"
}

⍝ expect the thunk to raise. If no error, throw (so the surrounding test fails).
∇ assertThrows(label;thunk) {
  r ← ⍞thunk catch ((:error, { data tag → "<<CAUGHT>>" }))
  (r ≡ "<<CAUGHT>>") {} { throw "assertThrows failed: no error (" , label , ")" }
}

⍝ run one named case. Records pass/fail and prints a line.
∇ test(name;thunk) {
  testTotal ← testTotal + 1
  r ← ⍞thunk catch ((:error, { data tag → "<<ERR>>" , data }))
  (r[0] ≡ "<<ERR>>") {
    testFail ← testFail + 1
    ⎕ ← (name , ": FAIL: " , (⍕r[1]))
  } else {
    testPass ← testPass + 1
    ⎕ ← (name , ": OK")
  }
}

⍝ print the machine-readable summary line the host parses for the CI exit code.
∇ testSummary() {
  ⎕ ← ("TESTS total=" , (⍕testTotal) , " pass=" , (⍕testPass) , " fail=" , (⍕testFail))
}
